[38;2;124;124;124mActually the standard result: if A ⊆ B and both are integral domains, char A = [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mchar B. The characteristic of an integral domain is the characteristic of its [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mprime subring, which is either ℤ or ℤ/pℤ. The prime subring is the subring [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mgenerated by 1.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mBut wait, does A ⊆ B mean 1_A = 1_B? If A is a subring of B sharing the same [0m
[38;2;124;124;124midentity, then yes. Usually "A ⊆ B where A and B are integral domains" with the [0m[38;2;124;124;124msubset relation implies A is a subring of B with the same identity.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me prove it carefully.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mDefinition: char R = smallest positive n with n·1_R = 0, or 0 if none.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSince A ⊆ B and they share identity (1_A = 1_B = 1), for any n, n·1 = 0 in A iff[0m[38;2;124;124;124mn·1 = 0 in B (since the element n·1 is the same element, and 0 is the same 0). [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mTherefore the set of n with n·1=0 is the same in both, so the characteristic is [0m[38;2;124;124;124mthe same.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually even if 1_A ≠ 1_B, we need to be careful. But the standard [0m
